"\n\n\n\n\n## Review\nThis work is set in a future era where mankind has overcome death.  \nThe world is perfectly governed by an omnipotent artificial intelligence called Thunderhead created by mankind.  \nIt is the ultimate utopia we have dreamed of.  \n\nBut in this perfect world, a dark necessary evil exists.  \nArtificial death becomes necessary to prevent overpopulation, and the beings who execute such deaths are Scythes.  \n\nThe , the only organization outside the rule of the Thunderhead, seems to be the only dystopian element in the utopian world.  \nHowever, this is not a failure of utopia, but an inevitable byproduct of its success.  \n\nThe utopian solution of perfect immortality inevitably caused the dystopian problem of artificial death.  \n\nThe Scythedom is operated under a strict legal system called the Ten Commandments.  \nAmong them, the commandment  seems to be the most difficult but most important commandment for scythes.  \n\nThe most fatal flaw of the Tenth Commandment system is the clause .  \nThis clause was intended to ensure the independence of the reapers for the sacred mission of harvesting, but in reality, it completely separates them from all laws and surveillance of the Thunderhead and human society, creating a potential lawless zone.  \n\nI should read the next series Thunderhead right away.\n"